# Memory Management { .heading-no-number }

> For design rationale, see [Perceus & FBIP Concepts](../concepts/perceus-and-fbip.html).
> This guide covers practical FBIP programming patterns.

Details on how Koka optimizes memory through reference counting and FBIP.

## Perceus GC
Perceus is a precise reference counting garbage collection technique. It is efficient, predictable, and allows for specialized optimizations like **FBIP**.

## FBIP (Functional But In-Place)
FBIP is a unique strength of Koka. It allows you to write purely functional code that the compiler automatically transforms into efficient in-place updates when objects are uniquely owned.
The `fip` keyword can be used to ensure that a function does not allocate or deallocate memory (if non-borrowed parameters are unique) and uses constant stack space.

Just like tail-call optimization lets you describe loops with regular function calls, FBIP lets you describe many in-place imperative algorithms with ordinary functional pattern matching and reconstruction.

### Example: Reversing a list in-place
If the input list `xs` is unique at runtime, Each `Cons` cell is reused and updated in-place:
```koka
fip fun rev-acc( xs : list<a>, acc : list<a> ) : list<a>
  match xs
    Cons(x,xx) -> rev-acc(xx, Cons(x,acc))
    Nil        -> acc
fip fun rev( xs : list<a> ) : list<a>
  rev-acc(xs,[])
```

### Allocation Bound: `fip(n)`
You can use `fip(n)` to allow a function to allocate at most `n` constructors. This is useful for operations like tree insertion where you might need to allocate exactly one new node.
```koka
type tree<k>
  Tip
  Bin(left: tree<k>, key: k, right: tree<k>)

fip(1) fun insert( t : tree<int>, k : int ) : tree<int>
  match t
    Bin(l,x,r) -> if (x==k) then Bin(l,k,r)
                  elif (x < k) then Bin(l,x,insert(r,k))
                  else Bin(insert(l,k),x,r)
    Tip        -> Bin(Tip,k,Tip)
```

This is the same style used by more advanced tree algorithms such as balanced insertion into red-black trees:
for a unique tree, only the genuinely new spine needs allocation, while matched nodes of the same size can be reused in place.

For shared or persistent trees, the same functional program adapts gracefully by copying just the shared path rather than the entire structure.

### Red-Black Tree Rebalancing

The sample `samples/basic/rbtree.kk` is a canonical FBIP case study. A red-black tree maintains two core invariants:

- every root-to-leaf path contains the same number of black nodes
- a red node never has a red child

Functional insertion preserves these invariants with local rebuilding and balancing in the style popularized by Okasaki.
In outline:

```unchecked
type color
  Red
  Black

type tree
  Leaf
  Node(color: color, left: tree, key: int, value: bool, right: tree)
```

```unchecked
fun balance-left( l : tree, k : int, v : bool, r : tree ): tree
  match l
    Node(_, Node(Red, lx, kx, vx, rx), ky, vy, ry)
      -> Node(Red, Node(Black, lx, kx, vx, rx), ky, vy, Node(Black, ry, k, v, r))
    ...
```

After inlining the balancing helpers, the crucial FBIP property becomes visible: each matched `Node` corresponds to a
rebuilt `Node` of the same size. In the unique case, those nodes can be reused in place. In the persistent case, only the
shared spine is copied, and unshared subtrees still reuse their existing storage.

### Morris Traversal via an Explicit Visitor

An in-order traversal like

```unchecked
fun tmap-inorder( t : tree, f : int -> int ) : tree
  match t
    Bin(l,x,r) -> Bin( l.tmap-inorder(f), f(x), r.tmap-inorder(f) )
    Tip        -> Tip
```

already benefits from reuse when `t` is unique, but it is not tail-recursive and therefore still uses stack space.

The Morris-traversal tradition asks for an in-order walk with no extra stack or heap. In imperative form this is usually
done by temporarily threading the tree. The FBIP version keeps the same idea but makes the pending traversal state
explicit as a visitor structure instead of implicit pointer rewiring:

```unchecked
type visitor
  Done
  BinR( right : tree, value : int, visit : visitor )
  BinL( left  : tree, value : int, visit : visitor )

type direction
  Up
  Down
```

This visitor is zipper-like: it represents the rest of the traversal after focusing on the current subtree.

We start with an empty visitor and walk downward:

```unchecked
fun tmap( f : int -> int, t : tree, visit : visitor, d : direction )
  match d
    Down -> match t
      Bin(l,x,r) -> tmap(f,l,BinR(r,x,visit),Down)  // A
      Tip        -> tmap(f,Tip,visit,Up)            // B
    Up -> match visit
      Done        -> t                              // C
      BinR(r,x,v) -> tmap(f,r,BinL(t,f(x),v),Down) // D
      BinL(l,x,v) -> tmap(f,Bin(l,x,t),v,Up)       // E
```

Operationally:

- `A`: descend the left spine and remember the right subtree in `BinR`
- `B`: once the left spine ends, start moving upward
- `C`: when the visitor is `Done`, the traversal is complete
- `D`: visit the saved node value in-order and switch to a `BinL` state while traversing the right subtree
- `E`: rebuild the tree node and continue upward

This is the key same-size transition chain for reuse analysis:

- `Bin` -> `BinR`
- `BinR` -> `BinL`
- `BinL` -> `Bin`

Because each step preserves size, a unique tree can be updated in place with no extra heap allocation. And because the
calls are tail calls, the compiled fast path also avoids extra traversal stack space.

### Reading Reuse Opportunities

The key thing to look for is whether a branch pattern-matches a constructor and then allocates a constructor of the same size in the result.
When that happens, Perceus can often overlay the new node onto the old one in the unique case.

This idea also applies to traversal-style programs. A tree walk that looks recursive in source may still compile to a tight loop once reuse analysis and TRMC have simplified the unique fast path.

## FBIP vs FIP

- `fip`: No allocation/deallocation, constant stack.

- `fbip`: No allocation, but allows deallocation and arbitrary stack space.
For more details, see the paper [FP^2: Fully in-Place Functional Programming](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/uploads/prod/2023/05/fip-tr-v2.pdf).

